---
title: Build a Branded Careers Page and Custom Application Form in Minutes
publishedAt: 2026-10-21
type: companies
---
**Quick answer:** Every company on GetHired can publish a branded careers page at its own address, such as gethired.devhub.co.in/careers/your-company. It lists all your open roles with search and filters, and includes your logo, cover image, About section, perks and custom sections. Each job can also have a custom application form with typed questions, optional or required fields, help text and private knockout rules.

A careers page is often the first thing a candidate checks after seeing your job post. For a startup without a web team, building one usually means a static page that's always out of date, or an ATS subscription you don't need yet.

## Your careers page

### Claim your address

Go to **Settings → Careers page** and pick a slug: `/careers/acme`, for example. It's 3 to 40 characters of lowercase letters, numbers and hyphens. If you change it later, the old address redirects to the new one, so links you've already shared keep working.

### Design the header

Your page header uses:

- your logo and company name;
- a tag line;
- an optional cover image;
- your website and social links;
- headquarters, company size and industry.

### Add content sections

Choose which sections to show and in what order:

- **About:** what you do and why it matters.
- **Why join us:** up to 12 perks, each with a title and a short description, such as "Remote-first", "Learning budget" or "ESOPs for everyone".
- **Custom sections:** up to three more, such as "How we hire", "Our engineering culture" or "Life in Bengaluru".

Preview the page before you publish it, and unpublish it at any time.

### A jobs list candidates can actually search

The jobs section works like the main [GetHired job search](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/jobs):

- keyword search over job titles and descriptions;
- filters for location, job type, workplace type (on-site, hybrid or remote), experience and minimum salary;
- sorting by newest or title.

Filters are kept in the address, so you can link straight to "all remote engineering roles" from your website or a tweet. Each job opens to its full description and the apply flow. The page updates the moment you publish, edit or close a job.

## Custom application forms

The default application form is quick for candidates, but it doesn't tell you much. With the form builder you decide exactly what to ask.

### Question types

- short text and long text;
- single choice and multiple choice;
- yes/no;
- number;
- date;
- URL.

Each question can be **required or optional** and can have **help text** that explains what you're looking for. You can also choose which standard fields to show.

### Question sets

Save a group of questions as a **question set** in **Settings → Question sets** and reuse it across jobs. "Backend basics" or "Design portfolio" can be added to a new job in one click.

### Knockout rules

Some requirements are firm: the right to work in a country, willingness to relocate, or availability to start within a notice period. For yes/no and choice questions you can add a **knockout rule**, for example "Are you able to work from our Pune office three days a week? → No → reject".

A few things to know:

- **Applicants never see the rules.** Rules are stored separately from the public form, so they don't appear on the careers page, in the API or anywhere an applicant can read.
- **It's your rule, not the AI's.** Knockouts only apply the exact condition you wrote.
- **Candidates aren't told instantly.** A knocked-out application moves to Rejected, and the rejection email is sent 24 hours later.
- **Use them sparingly.** A knockout should be a true requirement of the role, not a preference.

### Fair questions only

Question text is checked when you save it. Questions about protected characteristics, such as age, marital status or religion, are refused. See [fair AI hiring on GetHired](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/fair-ai-hiring-how-gethired-keeps-bias-out-of-candidate-screening).

### Answers you can filter

Because answers are typed, you can filter applicants by them: "only people who answered Yes to relocation" or "notice period under 30 days". The answers also feed into [AI applicant screening](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/ai-applicant-screening-how-to-sort-200-applicants-by-fit-without-reading-them-all), which grades each answer.

## Tips for a careers page that converts

1. **Be specific in "Why join us".** "Ship to 2 million users in your first month" is better than "fast-paced environment".
2. **Show salary ranges on jobs.** Posts with salaries get more and better applicants.
3. **Keep forms short.** Three to five questions is enough. Every extra required question loses some applicants.
4. **Explain your process** in a custom section: how many rounds, how long it takes, and whether there's an [AI screening interview](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/ai-screening-interviews-replace-30-phone-screens-with-one-page-reports).
5. **Link to it everywhere:** your website footer, LinkedIn page and email signature.

[Create your company account](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/auth/sign-up?company=true) and publish your careers page today.
